Downsampling, cluster half — rollups materialise on the compactor (#64, §18.6)
2026-08-24 · router + ingester×2 + querier×2 + compactor + one S3 bucket, NO
             all-node (deploy/compose/timelakedb-cluster-s3.yml)
             driven by deploy/compose/cluster-drill/rollup_cluster_drill.sh
================================================================================

THE CLAIM UNDER TEST: a role-split cluster downsamples correctly. There is no
`all`-node, so the COMPACTOR materialises the rollup — reading the source
through the same shard union a querier does (it holds the ingesters as peers
and reuses RemoteBuffers + the querier tail), writing the target back to the
shared store through an internal write that bypasses its read-only guard.

What a green run shows:
  - the compactor comes up in the cluster (shard_count=1, a lone compactor
    owning every rollup) and reads the ingesters' union;
  - a write through the router shards to an ingester; the compactor seals the
    aged-out buckets and writes rollup rows (rows_written > 0) with no
    all-node anywhere;
  - the target read back THROUGH A QUERIER is exact: one row per host, the
    right avg/count, and the counts sum to every source row — none dropped
    (a missed shard) or doubled (two materialisers);
  - a second pass writes nothing and the target is unchanged (exactly-once
    holds through the cluster).

